#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65674cef-349e-53ba-9d3b-003cfa1ca839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/90571cebb5f46697204869a7dbd07e83036d4fe92a5f46045bf6279b3b307999.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389099} {:answer/id #uuid "06c89610-14b3-5554-aa9f-e51bce286bc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e7/e718cdc16345d0da40874901ed4dc276c5cf33a75949365b59129a7bb934a8c1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389100} {:answer/id #uuid "1edeeca0-4ca4-5cf6-84ff-84ade5a12db7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/10a9d70717e657082fd8c36dc98876fcea53297ce79b44585574efef821dba51.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389101} {:answer/id #uuid "727029c5-1b11-518c-8047-594952cc6c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9d/9d781ed8813991d36d3da4802efa94543f1ee3fc542e1b8c72ff7ad521974e58.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389102} {:answer/id #uuid "853b32d5-c84a-5ebe-8783-2a507609eb35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8e3b059ba260063bed9924a2d9ebf2894d848b2a38781cf45e1ce7a365562e6d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "853b32d5-c84a-5ebe-8783-2a507609eb35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8e3b059ba260063bed9924a2d9ebf2894d848b2a38781cf45e1ce7a365562e6d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389103} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ee8e133-63d6-556c-8c7e-bf88612f2e87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0aaf351a-049f-501d-8313-1944db65783b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160928" :question/problem "![](images/85/85e8422a0b3421db73d8cbe88ef3c51b59f628f233d48982840a21c4d26bd26f.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=4$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=4$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=4,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e3b059ba260063bed9924a2d9ebf2894d848b2a38781cf45e1ce7a365562e6d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be52c672-2de5-56f0-9576-cf2f1cb2c3c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df47e830c98a8f5e3ddfdf909ec23163735b21486eb25d784bb6b88ea95e4f60.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389106} {:answer/id #uuid "282b979d-0b3b-5d2d-b38c-80725bf830b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/772ffafe5ebbe9aad24464f1a4ee7937c2a14895f62d6a73e0f1422a0bc899ee.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389107} {:answer/id #uuid "4892dc8f-6ac8-516f-82fe-ebb19d4650d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/2051bbb1a83dca354f57ff21a1e7208d9acbd9b4122ba8e7406933b449199b52.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389108} {:answer/id #uuid "3206686d-f296-5283-8260-05891a5656e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389109} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbd466a-6afa-5c79-99ef-7a3166b990c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e8/e8ed422a10846fc3f44fb894d1051f184dde24b5a38181f25366ba209c5986c5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4892dc8f-6ac8-516f-82fe-ebb19d4650d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/2051bbb1a83dca354f57ff21a1e7208d9acbd9b4122ba8e7406933b449199b52.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389108} :answer-field/id #uuid "62e79051-6fca-5a52-820a-2af4080980ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c0c66a8-0dcc-5684-892c-2aa4d891f439" :question/math-academy-id "q-160944" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e0e9b7d2cbff011a5ff209f335fb3d5e86af02a58c4ea5c8a8d46bd384e6a0c.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the right side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the right side.\n\nFor the right side, we have $u=\\sqrt{3}$ and $0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/20/2051bbb1a83dca354f57ff21a1e7208d9acbd9b4122ba8e7406933b449199b52.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8518316-52de-5ba5-901a-88b599ed820c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/67/67efdc05dfb99f81f4d612302e44546c114d84fc7e7b64e068c37b2d99ed6727.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389113} {:answer/id #uuid "64eb0677-5235-53b6-b7cd-0788ae0aeba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ed/ed4c3e91d019f95cdbb69df855a01b744b7ca016558e5ec67a8fe5efa6d02c59.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389114} {:answer/id #uuid "24a1914b-2de5-5ed4-93c5-b5ecb71587e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/59/59b8cc960c2a923a31ba1dfa8fd62d7b5697bc152623e6f295efb65bea2f6741.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389115} {:answer/id #uuid "3703cd30-32d4-5473-8372-73619c81d2e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389116} {:answer/id #uuid "8dabf549-e7c8-5353-a192-71adc1bb7137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9a/9a6f6dbe77adc95ad0ff833651fc22e2c68e5f0e541b65bc6ce7dcf7b7b41f4b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64eb0677-5235-53b6-b7cd-0788ae0aeba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ed/ed4c3e91d019f95cdbb69df855a01b744b7ca016558e5ec67a8fe5efa6d02c59.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389114} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a9f3a02-934a-5c67-a36e-a5daf2200dc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29377ed0-f103-5d84-ae34-1182af962bec" :question/math-academy-id "q-160948" :question/problem "![](images/93/930509fb9224152be07ef3e14550e0f7afe803428a357963eb3796e0650685a3.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(1\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,1\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the second and third quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed4c3e91d019f95cdbb69df855a01b744b7ca016558e5ec67a8fe5efa6d02c59.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0345274-f2c4-5705-a32d-e15314701def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/ee80e889e142c56b48a884a71dd714349ffbb95a6bdee3a3406679ed1edfaaa3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389120} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ace1d8-0fe6-5683-95d7-9cad14abffa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e5/e50d218c70d9f4f5e77a9ef53b5fb2712b6b400450fc2165e7972b06621b9757.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389121} {:answer/id #uuid "04525aaf-6e10-5c32-b37f-1661336ebc54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389122} {:answer/id #uuid "0cf22586-b6de-5257-8078-913f559b060a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/aa/aafdff659d5c73ab37dfe9cf27c8777d03d7731f04dd106c149e6b4bfbc45fb2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389123} {:answer/id #uuid "55b60f0e-fc18-56fe-b9cf-de221f61768b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/39/399dc3c9750ed7e041f1973cad0a24a18903360ba68f0dbcb42c566436c9e259.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0345274-f2c4-5705-a32d-e15314701def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/ee80e889e142c56b48a884a71dd714349ffbb95a6bdee3a3406679ed1edfaaa3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389120} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f3b4e13-9ace-5415-af28-7000cb517d7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7dffa96-ac1f-5c9d-b16b-8ea8f986a8f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161025" :question/problem "![](images/de/de9f7a32a41bcecc1707b90d6818d58473473a4dc26d17e39e957461de95d671.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{6},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{12}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and $0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ with the positive $x$-axis, where one endpoint is the point $O,$ while the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $\\sqrt{6}$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ee/ee80e889e142c56b48a884a71dd714349ffbb95a6bdee3a3406679ed1edfaaa3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38fd4660-39ca-585f-8e3a-5d8b569be77d" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00aa38ea-bac0-52a0-86dc-bc6b9414c3ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389127} {:answer/id #uuid "d3398719-8fe7-5303-a753-c43f72bb0596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{16}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389128} {:answer/id #uuid "b175c8ee-c27d-5255-8737-063a160f1607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{16}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(r+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389129} {:answer/id #uuid "1a083a94-f096-531d-ba65-44631b40cc71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389130} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca6e142-ca44-5238-9857-d72f2dd5f577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(r+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00aa38ea-bac0-52a0-86dc-bc6b9414c3ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389127} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c650825-40f2-5963-96ba-a65375cf218b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a368d06f-dd38-5739-a1e1-1669cc437d2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161041" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b077d20bcb79f293383a7810d1c46ad42ce78d17a8d631b01f66a9fd547f475.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is ${∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A$ expressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1)r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1)\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eae7164-44be-5daa-8f60-01f666ee85e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)^{2}\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389134} {:answer/id #uuid "93dea69d-f320-5150-ac1d-c87cdb24a44f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{y}^{2}\\sqrt{2+{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389135} {:answer/id #uuid "4a911b97-1499-505c-8f0a-ed568989c26a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)^{2}\\sqrt{1+3{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389136} {:answer/id #uuid "35902b8d-147e-540a-a558-cc7891a3ea21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+3{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389137} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc31078-436e-59f2-b481-be085183ca63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cc31078-436e-59f2-b481-be085183ca63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389138} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ffeb77b-3aa9-525c-934d-f2444652b366" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0abbafda-b663-58bc-a5e8-afa7f578ba96" :question/math-academy-id "q-161091" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x={y}^{3}+1$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x={y}^{3}+1\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={y}^{3}+1,$ and\n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y}\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′},\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f{f}^{′})^{2}+(f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(y)={y}^{3}+1,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(y)=3{y}^{2}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+(3{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af7bb4e7-5a7e-5614-af5c-b0557486f506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389140} {:answer/id #uuid "217657c7-12a1-539f-a238-a933de3cb8bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389141} {:answer/id #uuid "a7df0e0a-4e17-5d25-abd4-def686f904bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389142} {:answer/id #uuid "b38005c6-8f6d-54a6-8226-eb2b6a6a837e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389143} {:answer/id #uuid "83c1f7c4-9d23-598c-b5ad-74e8cd9c6a42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b38005c6-8f6d-54a6-8226-eb2b6a6a837e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389143} :answer-field/id #uuid "54ef5f39-9801-5bd2-b219-adbef51cdebb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "929a0232-4773-59cb-9c62-800f13322539" :question/math-academy-id "q-161095" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y={x}^{4}$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [-2,2].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y={x}^{4}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [-2,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={x}^{4},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f{f}^{′})^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={x}^{4},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=4{x}^{3}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+(4{x}^{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f764ef66-80ba-5dd4-95f0-f40227cffaa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389147} {:answer/id #uuid "050352de-c1bd-5d67-9579-ebdbd025a455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389148} {:answer/id #uuid "482b7783-c529-524c-a098-a22acb66ee43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389149} {:answer/id #uuid "92fa62f8-7504-56f9-8cec-7b7eb2d858e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389150} {:answer/id #uuid "71e856e7-0056-5715-808a-11df70aa0fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92fa62f8-7504-56f9-8cec-7b7eb2d858e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389150} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b233718-f0ae-5430-bf3b-0ba9d8e47c07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f6d9f66-8b90-5e43-ac23-53d484fde86e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161100" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6ddc0b2138025b07ffdb7bf34afabeba578400b194922bec7a733cc9246c7c0f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2$ and the line $y=2-x,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(1,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2 & =2 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ r(r-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$\n\n- For $y=2-x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-x \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(2,0)$ and $(0,2).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/58/587c299c1bdf89061be205cf185d6335fba865d2e30c81cac5b9d223fc84b486.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\le r\\le 2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ),\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2/(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given Its Image Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a4f3877-28ec-525d-bc7b-fb086bbcf483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f9/f97f1344d6c3a0fd5cfbe06057193e4fe46f9d368cf32cc174db52ba94243744.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389154} {:answer/id #uuid "c056697a-8b8b-503a-9760-b2880703e4cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/aa/aaa000e6d8798b9f62cc3afc814931378dee78f1527e46befb5cfd51b0a00249.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389155} {:answer/id #uuid "c026f87d-478e-5c1f-bbc5-a89910646e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4d/4d1341dc970cd8a7c52ee7e0988e40f40254ddb8dee77b7dcba2010a126f9fe0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389156} {:answer/id #uuid "030daf68-f271-5761-bbf4-91a4ff37750b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/83/83d410ca6de599371a47574cf2c6c3fa659f89dca32c6aadd5596276c3ef0c9b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389157} {:answer/id #uuid "cb82fd7c-a45a-557f-b1c5-bab2e976e278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6f/6fd834262bbe36779afee02caa6df26520fb4d7e3c451806411e7c2a34ba0ab8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a4f3877-28ec-525d-bc7b-fb086bbcf483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f9/f97f1344d6c3a0fd5cfbe06057193e4fe46f9d368cf32cc174db52ba94243744.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389154} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0491ffc-dbaa-5a2b-9802-8000e5fa5ce9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc6f6478-43eb-5df8-9592-d3846d859923" :question/math-academy-id "q-161113" :question/problem "![](images/22/22ed424a27d1674bc7f4316b0b644e3dbac4c9f460e23ea2aa6ee90cb74b4c5b.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f9/f97f1344d6c3a0fd5cfbe06057193e4fe46f9d368cf32cc174db52ba94243744.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given Its Image Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8e49242-5f05-594e-879e-d014e1481c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/81/81d08b5c3f511d6a51c1fc01d40ef25cf52b1fc8f10c055f924425aab7f9e39c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389161} {:answer/id #uuid "5d15ed38-8317-574a-8f57-c7ef6444372d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8a/8a8b84b7858341f49fdba9e64c13f0544cce74c8f8c6128527f9b42e9c3bb718.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389162} {:answer/id #uuid "81c25f98-84c7-57e9-bb8b-9cd6e1af76e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/89/89f8cd5fdb19ae5a3eb74bb1d519da855ea6f32027aa3e536c8cea99228c13cb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389163} {:answer/id #uuid "186ec206-6c76-5992-b7ad-40bb2541f704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/88/88d58291666ed60113e3d16c5bc30e6d4375cbae3501f8e3e26bd9bbacb4940d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389164} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ab65d8-b72e-5eee-a7fe-4d1446facdc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8f/8f0573ca9a95fc277d1993e08b7307d2a9e8be48b80e1ea2360f9fc688b440b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "186ec206-6c76-5992-b7ad-40bb2541f704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/88/88d58291666ed60113e3d16c5bc30e6d4375cbae3501f8e3e26bd9bbacb4940d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389164} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f30debe-a057-56e2-b225-26c5a2263926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "087f8af8-ce2b-5e52-94ce-b9f44a5bd078" :question/math-academy-id "q-161116" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9cd25963ec1ae28860b821825597ca16074e3b0231bd83da7505d37405c23c2.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=2,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/88/88d58291666ed60113e3d16c5bc30e6d4375cbae3501f8e3e26bd9bbacb4940d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c0f4b8-0b77-5e8a-aae2-2d27ea125d82" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86459122-4bdd-512e-9e34-298272859e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{4}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389168} {:answer/id #uuid "20db1bb1-9d9a-5010-b5a7-6117b0a95c14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+y+xy}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389169} {:answer/id #uuid "1457b555-cd36-5515-a457-4a89ad7bf36a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}+4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389170} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd6e4de-d5b0-5620-a4d8-71f31bfdbd8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}+2xy}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389171} {:answer/id #uuid "b5bd6ab0-9645-5100-a44f-fc36a1a0120c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}1+{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1457b555-cd36-5515-a457-4a89ad7bf36a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}+4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389170} :answer-field/id #uuid "43daddd4-5f4f-51e5-ba06-33b9f5b182c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c26fb9d1-bd64-5afc-83db-47655a4115bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161138" :question/problem "Consider the portion of the surface $z=x{y}^{2}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y={x}^{2},$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=x{y}^{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/14/1419b067fe92945249655c5d29498aa4d3b0269d79ebd237fd9ed2a713b70b6a.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})={y}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})=2xy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{({y}^{2})}^{2}+(2xy)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}+4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}+4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c0f4b8-0b77-5e8a-aae2-2d27ea125d82" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5d540b0-4408-5667-a0db-a90347a718b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+(y+1){e}^{x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389175} {:answer/id #uuid "37b76347-5859-5d6e-8b0f-ab07a22b98cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+(y+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389176} {:answer/id #uuid "d986df6b-5639-5102-8fd7-9a29abdd7669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389177} {:answer/id #uuid "86209031-deeb-5c6b-ae0a-144a5367c604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389178} {:answer/id #uuid "a63c61fd-3721-53ba-9d5b-95914b2da4f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}1+{y}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d986df6b-5639-5102-8fd7-9a29abdd7669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389177} :answer-field/id #uuid "f23fcbc4-3abe-5fd3-bdce-8e4f9b6793af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc263672-04e3-59a9-9f18-ccb933eb5e00" :question/math-academy-id "q-161141" :question/problem "Consider the portion of the surface $z=y{e}^{x}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by the lines $y=2x,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=y{e}^{x},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/90/903b7f3c6bd9721a974160487ca5099fdf54ce02b61697a7830bb662b26d8953.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{x})=y{e}^{x}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(y{e}^{x})^{2}+({e}^{x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+({y}^{2}+1){e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d3a0d58-20a3-570d-91e7-aa8f10dafb5a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Triple Integral as a Repeated Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c676d4a4-418b-5d4a-b805-948c3d43044d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}+xy+{z}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389182} {:answer/id #uuid "df4807f8-6afd-571a-8c8e-c6b0dc184f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{2}+xy+{z}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389183} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b9a8d8-3e11-5b1e-b965-c4f5e8893dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{x}^{2}+xy+{z}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389184} {:answer/id #uuid "9ece1154-32b4-5bbc-8f11-81891db833a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}+xy+{z}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389185} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa9da43-5b93-50a6-a625-5c6149bec690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{x}^{2}+xy+{z}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c676d4a4-418b-5d4a-b805-948c3d43044d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}+xy+{z}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389182} :answer-field/id #uuid "28d33470-5ce6-52e9-af34-2189795442c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "825a1b17-7e35-53ff-bd30-0a045f2e4adc" :question/math-academy-id "q-161337" :question/problem "Express the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+xy+{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the box\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 5}\n$$\n\nas a repeated integral of the form \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{e}^{f}…\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given the limits for $x,y,$ and $z,$ we can express our triple integral as a repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+xy+{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}+xy+{z}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3486150-b3c2-5faf-99d5-b42ced350996" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Over a Surface as a Repeated Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9d8774c-14c0-5481-9a1f-1c3acd63867a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u^{2} + v^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194298} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ce5f03-88a7-5102-bfd3-b0e4a75a4aed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u^{2} - v^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194299} {:answer/id #uuid "31b2be0c-93a0-5cd7-8143-0596b46129e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u^{2} + uv + v^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194300} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce44ca0-320e-5c90-a723-d02a6010576d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194301} {:answer/id #uuid "7423bf6e-47f3-548e-afbe-feee4e861dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(u + v)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194302}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5503c223-e87a-5159-abb7-434053fd7b58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194297}] :question/id #uuid "a3bcb9d3-050c-5ab9-bd2a-436465223147" :question/math-academy-id "q-161360" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨x^{2}, y^{2}, z^{2}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z = x + y$ for $0 \\le x \\le 2$ and $0 \\le y \\le 2$. Using the parametrization $x = u$ and $y = v$, the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}AAAAAAAdvdu$.\nWhat is the missing expression?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9ef2524-c316-5f5c-bb17-9410d6df7855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u = 2v$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126662} {:answer/id #uuid "af21f9b2-947b-5b4b-9b83-b2324aabe6c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u =-w^{2}$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126663} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ef1406-0abe-5cfe-bc04-7ae04a77ecf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u = w$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126664} {:answer/id #uuid "e90dd075-4fb2-51bb-9367-44b42c15619d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw -$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126665} {:answer/id #uuid "0cffeeef-f100-51b5-ac9e-aa76adbf7807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u = 2w^{2}$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126666}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4d271abc-b1d9-5284-9062-68834149c800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126661}] :question/id #uuid "685e989d-8721-5a8c-a4d8-0e44efe4bc9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161819" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T$, where $T$ is defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} - w^{2} \\\\ we^{u} \\\\ u - v\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "659a692b-2aae-5acc-a235-560ceb57bafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389189} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c0fc1d-78bf-56a3-bddf-42bd659ad7fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/30/3033f0ae73e3f484fc5d3d65e388062feba1824fb3da9971d560cf2f518f49bb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389190} {:answer/id #uuid "404681f6-e3e2-5a85-a1a6-1e1e580bf865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d5889bd5e8d537757de25a7b1a868ff8a903da14c9088c5f8de5a70d8b078552.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389191} {:answer/id #uuid "121673a3-bbfa-573d-a9d1-f20fe0e8e765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389192} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e134b4-7103-5125-b7fc-086313478261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b0/b0171dfd42295e39cd45186e8edba3a5105fbd1bd02a1621223091de75815f61.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "121673a3-bbfa-573d-a9d1-f20fe0e8e765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389192} :answer-field/id #uuid "3825278d-de78-5afb-82f2-adfd041f336b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2f0da55-1926-59a4-96c8-e68855af7a42" :question/math-academy-id "q-161837" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2} & 1 \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={u}^{3}-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}-v & <0 \\\\ v & >{u}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv={u}^{3}\n$$\n\ndefines a cubic function, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/cb/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "773f7224-683a-5657-93fd-0b49bba9ddc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389196} {:answer/id #uuid "3223453f-3db3-5cf7-9170-0776a21f7b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8b35fbafaf8d121bceef7a0763253df075fe4fe85eb3c992f95c59d05f986c67.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389197} {:answer/id #uuid "d90c24c3-b6bf-58dc-bb07-e1245dcf6b0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/09f7f36ecc2197cc95e1878fa22ac22367978809a5241f09886a7eb2fab071f3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389198} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae8e4b1-ec09-543b-b30d-54e69ad80dcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/105e6e8a010b7b9001f75cf17e11cbbc1e031d83cbc2ddfafc3595c745106f3f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389199} {:answer/id #uuid "682bc6d2-be17-5ccb-842c-4b844ea68ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/090bab0ac1c6356010d83a6ad54c59b53b7dd12062fb5e331a936ce3f3a1b411.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3223453f-3db3-5cf7-9170-0776a21f7b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8b35fbafaf8d121bceef7a0763253df075fe4fe85eb3c992f95c59d05f986c67.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389197} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd81eba-4d1a-5e26-b6d5-47144737edcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99972da5-e4e5-5505-b6d1-ad2a467bcb50" :question/math-academy-id "q-161839" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ uv+1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ uv+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-v\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{u}^{2}+v & >0 \\\\ v & >-2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-2{u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/8b/8b35fbafaf8d121bceef7a0763253df075fe4fe85eb3c992f95c59d05f986c67.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2407dcd5-06ff-568b-bb6d-b2494e2bd942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389203} {:answer/id #uuid "07712d2e-2b92-521c-b6ae-d43d00f464d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/13/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389204} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3ba601-6388-5b1c-ac01-5522063f4425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fd/fd2a51ca3a1654c2e857018268bf251366472a2c5d7f90009d5ccfbf1b1edd9d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389205} {:answer/id #uuid "dc797ebb-78cb-5a5b-ac03-97f75fe59dce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389206} {:answer/id #uuid "2201cc6c-a008-5122-982f-2699a2c582b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/95bf5e059fda0fc1ba8423b35fffa57d9cae0cc2a2a7e40b0c2539b6a7ae7678.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2407dcd5-06ff-568b-bb6d-b2494e2bd942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389203} :answer-field/id #uuid "644861a4-b1bb-5e40-b887-ad0f73e8386a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82d50c5e-c224-54bc-84fa-f5633f671803" :question/math-academy-id "q-161840" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (2v) \\\\  & =2u+2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u+2v & <0 \\\\ 2v & <-2u \\\\ v & <-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44718c67-0ba1-524e-9c98-36d26b5e3a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389210} {:answer/id #uuid "225446d3-d261-5650-bed9-eaac